ARTICLES = [
{"slug":"cftc-interim-final-rule-swap-definition-casino-style-gambling",
"title":"CFTC Rule Carves Casino-Style Gambling Out of 'Swap'",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"An interim final rule excludes licensed sportsbook and casino products from the swap definition, with comments due 18 November.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-21T06:10:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-states"],
"related_firms":["greenberg-traurig-llp","fox-rothschild-llp","holland-and-knight-llp"],
"body":[
"On 19 October 2026 the Commodity Futures Trading Commission published an interim final rule in the Federal Register that adds a new paragraph (7) to the definition of swap in 17 CFR 1.3. The rule, signed on 15 October and effective on publication, provides that a swap does not include certain gambling products offered under state or tribal gaming law, including sportsbook wagers, casino games and other licensed games of chance. Comments are due by 18 November 2026. Because the Commission issued the text without prior notice and comment, it remains open to modification, but it is operative law in the meantime.",
"The rule works through two cumulative tests. The provider test asks whether the offeror is licensed or authorised to offer gambling or accept wagers under state law, or under the law of a federally recognised tribe acting under the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act, and whether it is acting within that licence. The product test asks whether the product is traded on a board of trade, an organised exchange, a swap execution facility or any other venue where trades are executed multilaterally or negotiated individually. Wagers set by a house on standard terms will generally satisfy the product test, while contracts listed on a designated contract market will not.",
"The Commission presents the rule as a codification of a longstanding view rather than a change of position. It argues that gambling products lack the features that define swaps: they are typically bilateral, non-transferable, uncleared and not traded on an organised market. It draws an analogy to its 2012 exclusions for insurance and certain consumer and commercial agreements. It also frames the measure as protecting the federal-state balance, on the footing that state and tribal regimes will continue to govern traditional gambling.",
"The more contentious point is what the rule says about event contracts. By treating exchange-traded, centrally cleared event contracts as swaps, the Commission preserves its claim to exclusive jurisdiction over them, while confirming that a licensed sportsbook is outside that claim. The preamble cites the divergent decisions of the Sixth, Ninth and Third Circuits on whether sports event contracts are swaps, and says the rule is intended to reduce the confusion those conflicting decisions have caused. Whether it achieves that outcome will depend on how courts treat an agency definition adopted under the good-cause exception.",
"For licensed operators, the practical benefit is a clearer federal statement that sportsbook and iGaming offerings fall outside the Commodity Exchange Act. That has value in disputes where a counterparty or a prediction-market competitor argues that all sports wagering is, in substance, derivatives activity. It does not, however, alter state licensing obligations, and it does not shield an operator that blends exchange-style trading features into a gaming product. Operators with hybrid products should map each feature against the product test before relying on the exclusion.",
"For prediction-market platforms, the rule sharpens the choice between two regulatory identities. A platform that operates a designated contract market remains inside the CFTC perimeter and faces the preemption litigation that is already under way in several states. A platform that wishes to rely on the gaming exclusion must hold a state or tribal licence, which brings with it integrity, responsible gambling, tax and advertising obligations. The rule therefore increases the incentive for firms to decide which side of the line they are on, rather than occupying both.",
"Counsel should treat the 18 November comment deadline as a genuine opportunity. Points worth addressing include the scope of the provider test for operators licensed in some but not all jurisdictions in which they accept bets, the treatment of white-label and B2B arrangements, and the interaction with tribal compacts. Clients should also expect state attorneys general to cite the rule in pending enforcement actions, and should review any federal preemption arguments in current filings accordingly.",
"In sum, the interim final rule is a meaningful but not final development. It strengthens the position of licensed state and tribal operators, narrows the room for ambiguity about what the CFTC regulates, and raises the stakes of the pending circuit-level disputes. Operators, platforms and their advisers should monitor the comment docket and prepare for a possible revised text in the first half of 2027."
]},
{"slug":"australia-acma-notable-people-gambling-ad-ban-guidance-2027",
"title":"ACMA Guidance Defines 'Notable People' in Gambling Ads",
"category":"Compliance",
"excerpt":"Australia's regulator takes a deliberately broad view of who is covered by the new ban, which starts on 1 January 2027.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-21T07:25:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["australia"],
"related_firms":["pinsent-masons-llp","bird-and-bird"],
"body":[
"The Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA) has published initial industry guidance on the new prohibition on notable people appearing in gambling advertising, as reported on 20 October 2026. The prohibition, which was enacted through the Interactive Gambling Amendment Bill 2026 passed by Parliament in August, takes effect on 1 January 2027. The guidance is the first detailed signal of how the regulator intends to apply the concept, and it adopts a deliberately broad reading.",
"According to the guidance, a notable person includes current and former professional athletes, celebrities and social media influencers, and also other prominent individuals such as politicians, academics and business figures. The ACMA does not limit the concept to people who appeal to children. It notes that notable people may also appeal to those at risk of gambling harm and to groups such as younger men. There is no minimum follower count, and the regulator indicates that even an esports commentator with a modest following could qualify if they hold influence within a community.",
"Each case will be assessed on its merits, taking into account public recognition, achievements, media exposure and the person's ability to influence consumers. Operators should note that the status of a person is not fixed at the time a campaign is commissioned. If someone becomes famous during a campaign that was compliant at the outset, the guidance indicates that the operator must stop running the content. That places a continuing monitoring obligation on licensees and their agencies, which most contracts with talent and media partners do not currently contemplate.",
"The ban applies to licensed operators in Australia and covers advertising and sponsorship involving notable persons across television, radio, news websites, social media and other digital platforms. Responsibility rests with the operator, not with the individual who appears in the content. Horseracing advertising on dedicated racing channels is reported to be exempt, and the wider package also includes a blackout on gambling advertising during live sporting events and at sports venues.",
"Transitional treatment matters for sponsorship portfolios. Existing sponsorship agreements signed before 2 July 2026 may be protected until the end of 2031, according to the reporting on the guidance. Operators should therefore audit each arrangement by signing date, confirm which agreements fall inside the protected class, and document that analysis. Agreements signed after that date, and any renewal or material variation, should be assumed to be subject to the ban from 1 January 2027 unless advice indicates otherwise.",
"Contractual risk allocation is the immediate drafting priority. Talent agreements, affiliate and influencer arrangements, and media buying terms will need warranties on notable-person status, rights to suspend and withdraw content at short notice, and clear allocation of the cost of takedown. Operators should also revisit approval workflows so that a creative cannot be released without a recorded assessment against the ACMA criteria.",
"Because the test is open-textured, early engagement with the regulator is likely to be valuable. Operators with borderline cases, such as micro-influencers with niche communities or former athletes in broadcasting roles, may wish to seek informal views before committing spend. The guidance describes itself as initial, and further material should be expected as the start date approaches.",
"Overseas operators and their advisers should watch the Australian approach closely. A broad, outcome-based definition of who may front gambling advertising, combined with strict operator liability, is a template other regulators could adopt, and groups with multi-market advertising strategies may find it simpler to converge on the strictest standard rather than maintain separate creative libraries."
]},
{"slug":"ukgc-5-million-land-based-casino-settlement-aml-social-responsibility",
"title":"UKGC's £5m Casino Settlement: AML Lessons",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"Three national land-based casino operators will pay £5 million over AML and social responsibility failures.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-21T08:40:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-kingdom"],
"related_firms":["harris-hagan","wiggin-llp","mishcon-de-reya-llp"],
"body":[
"On 7 October 2026 the Gambling Commission announced that three nationwide land-based casino operators will pay a total of £5 million following an investigation that found failures in anti-money laundering (AML) controls and in social responsibility. The Commission's public summary does not set out the operators' identities or a full breakdown of the failings, so this analysis draws on what has been published and on the pattern of the Commission's earlier regulatory settlements.",
"The settlement continues a familiar enforcement theme. The Commission has repeatedly said that operators must understand the money laundering and terrorist financing risks they face, and must apply customer due diligence and source-of-funds checks that are proportionate to those risks. In casinos, the typical weaknesses identified in earlier cases have been inadequate risk assessments, thresholds that trigger enhanced checks too late, reliance on staff discretion without a documented rationale, and failure to act on information already held about a customer.",
"The pairing of AML and social responsibility failures is also characteristic. The two sets of obligations draw on overlapping data: a customer who spends heavily relative to known means should prompt both a financial-crime review and a customer-interaction review. Where a business treats those as separate silos, the Commission has tended to find that neither is effective. Operators should assess whether their escalation processes allow a single trigger to start both workstreams and whether decisions are recorded in a form that can be evidenced to the regulator.",
"A global settlement across three operators suggests a thematic or multi-operator review rather than a single incident. For legal advisers this matters because it increases the likelihood that other land-based licensees with similar controls will be asked for information. Firms that have not recently refreshed their business-wide risk assessment, or tested their controls against the Commission's published AML guidance for casinos, should consider doing so before any approach from the regulator.",
"The regulatory settlement route offers certainty and speed, and typically includes a payment to be used for purposes of public benefit, together with commitments on remedial action. Boards should note, however, that settlement does not prevent the Commission from taking later action if commitments are not met. Senior management accountability is also a recurring feature: personal management licence holders can be examined on what they knew and what they did, and a corporate settlement does not necessarily end individual exposure.",
"The announcement came alongside other recent Commission news, including the 30 September announcement of new standards intended to make gaming machines fairer and safer, and a prosecution outcome involving criminal funds derived from illegal poker. Taken together, these items indicate that the Commission is pursuing a broad programme across land-based, machine and illegal-gambling activity, and that AML is not confined to the online sector.",
"Practical steps for land-based licensees include refreshing the business risk assessment, reviewing thresholds for enhanced due diligence, retraining front-of-house staff on escalation, confirming that records of customer interactions are complete and searchable, and checking that third-party and group-wide arrangements do not leave gaps between venues. A short, privileged gap analysis against the Commission's published findings in recent cases is a proportionate first step.",
"Finally, licensees should consider disclosure. Where internal review identifies failings similar to those described by the Commission, the licence conditions on reporting key events and the Commission's expectations of openness are relevant. Early, well-prepared engagement with the regulator has consistently been treated more favourably than discovery through an investigation."
]},
{"slug":"new-york-gaming-commission-proposed-rules-at-risk-bettors-ai-ban",
"title":"New York Proposes Sportsbook Rules on At-Risk Bettors",
"category":"Compliance",
"excerpt":"Proposed rules would flag at-risk users, bar AI-tailored bonuses and require financial vulnerability checks, with a 60-day comment window.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-21T09:15:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-states"],
"related_firms":["duane-morris-llp","dickinson-wright-pllc","womble-bond-dickinson-us-llp"],
"body":[
"On 9 October 2026 the New York State Gaming Commission, chaired by Brian O'Dwyer, unanimously advanced proposed regulations that would add significant player-protection duties for licensed online sports wagering operators. The proposals follow plans first published in March 2026 and now enter a 60-day public comment period. Absent major changes, the rules would return to the Commission for adoption. The text is still a proposal and the final form may differ.",
"The first element concerns identification of at-risk bettors. Reporting on the proposal indicates that operators would use the threshold guidelines developed in New Jersey to flag users, and would then be required to check in with them. Illustrative triggers include deposits above $10,000 in 24 hours or wagers above $1 million in 90 days. Borrowing an existing benchmark from another regulated market lowers the design burden for multi-state operators, but it also means that operators should expect scrutiny of how they calibrate any additional internal triggers.",
"The second element is a prohibition on using artificial intelligence to tailor bonuses or wagers to individual users. This goes to the heart of personalised marketing, which many operators use to retain high-value customers. If adopted, the restriction would require a review of promotional engines, recommendation systems and any vendor tools that segment players on predicted value. The key drafting questions will be how the rule defines AI, whether rule-based segmentation is caught, and whether the ban applies to offers sent to all users in a segment or only to individually generated terms.",
"The third element is enhanced due diligence on financial vulnerability under defined triggers. One reported trigger is an increase of 50% or more in weekly app use compared with the previous two weeks. That is a behavioural, not a financial, signal, and it will pull data on engagement into the compliance function in a way that some operators have not previously done. Operators will need a documented methodology, a clear escalation path and a record of the action taken.",
"The proposals raise data protection and fairness considerations. Collecting information about a customer's finances to assess vulnerability must be reconciled with privacy notices, consent mechanisms and state privacy laws. Operators should also consider the risk of inconsistent treatment: a trigger-based system that is applied unevenly could expose the operator to complaints from customers who feel singled out, as well as to regulatory criticism if comparable customers are treated differently.",
"The comment period is the opportunity to shape the final rule. Issues that commenters may wish to raise include the practical definition of the check-in, the timing and form of any required contact, the retention of records, safe harbours for good-faith application of the thresholds, and a phased implementation period so that systems can be built and tested. Trade associations are likely to file, but operators with specific technical constraints may benefit from submitting their own comments.",
"The proposal also sits within a broader trend. Other US states have been moving from expansion to harm-reduction regulation, with proposals on advertising, microbetting, college props and tax. Operators active in several states should avoid building a New York-only solution and should instead design controls at group level, with state-specific parameters that can be adjusted.",
"In the near term, compliance teams should inventory their personalisation tools, map existing at-risk indicators against the proposed triggers, assess data availability and vendor capability, and prepare a cost and timing estimate for implementation. Boards in New York-licensed groups should expect to be asked how the business would comply if the rules are adopted substantially as proposed."
]},
{"slug":"missouri-prediction-market-cease-and-desist-novig-crypto-com-nba-props",
"title":"Missouri Fights Prediction Markets as NBA Limits Props",
"category":"Market Entry",
"excerpt":"Novig and Crypto.com sue over Missouri's cease-and-desist notices while the NBA and its sportsbook partners restrict certain player props.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-21T10:05:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-states"],
"related_firms":["fox-rothschild-llp","holland-and-knight-llp","greenberg-traurig-llp"],
"body":[
"Two developments reported in mid-October 2026 show how integrity concerns and jurisdictional conflict are converging in the US sports wagering market. In Missouri, prediction market operators are challenging the state's attempt to stop them from offering sports event contracts. In professional basketball, the NBA has agreed with its official sportsbook partners to restrict certain player prop markets ahead of the new season. Both bear directly on market-entry strategy.",
"In September 2026 the Missouri Attorney General, Catherine Hanaway, issued cease-and-desist notices to prediction market operators, requiring them to stop offering sports event contracts in the state or to obtain the necessary gambling licences. Novig and Crypto.com have since filed separate federal lawsuits, reportedly in the Eastern and Western Districts of Missouri respectively, seeking to prevent enforcement of state gambling law against their products. Both argue that their contracts are federally regulated and fall under the exclusive jurisdiction of the CFTC, so that federal commodities law preempts Missouri's restrictions.",
"The Eighth Circuit has not yet ruled on the question, and the wider dispute could eventually reach the Supreme Court. Cease-and-desist deadlines for six operators have reportedly expired, which leaves the state free to escalate. The legal position is also in flux because of the CFTC's recent interim final rule on the swap definition, which confirms that licensed gambling products are outside its swap jurisdiction while preserving its position on exchange-traded event contracts. Each side will read that development in its own favour.",
"The NBA's changes address a different risk. Reports of 16 and 19 October describe an agreement under which sportsbooks will stop accepting under bets on players expected to play fewer than 12 minutes, and will no longer allow combinations of multiple under bets on the same player's performance in a game. Further reports cite restrictions on parlays involving the under on a player's statistics and on first-of markets such as the opening tip or a player's first shot, rebound or assist. The stated motivation is the vulnerability of narrow props on reserve players to manipulation, following several integrity controversies.",
"These measures are commercial agreements with licensed partners, not regulation, but they have regulatory consequences. State commissions often adopt league requests on prohibited markets, and operators will need to confirm that restrictions are implemented consistently across states and in their trading, risk and customer-facing systems. Contracts with data and integrity providers may also need updating so that the restricted markets are identified in feeds and monitoring rules.",
"The reported gap is that comparable contracts may remain available on federally regulated prediction market platforms. The NBA is said to have held discussions with the CFTC but has not reached a formal agreement. For leagues, this creates an asymmetry in which the licensed sportsbook channel, subject to state oversight and league agreements, is more tightly controlled than an exchange-style channel. That asymmetry is an argument both for state regulators who want parity and for platforms who want clarity on which rules apply to them.",
"For operators evaluating entry or expansion, the practical advice is to treat licensing status as a strategic decision. Holding a state licence brings clear obligations but also access to a defined legal framework and protection from state enforcement. Operating through a federally regulated exchange offers national reach but exposes the business to state challenge and unresolved preemption risk. Groups that do both should ensure that their disclosures, marketing and risk allocations are consistent with each model.",
"Advisers should monitor the Missouri dockets, any Eighth Circuit activity, further state cease-and-desist actions and the CFTC comment process closing on 18 November. Contracts with sportsbook, data and league counterparties should be reviewed for change-in-law provisions, market-restriction mechanics and termination rights, so that clients can respond quickly to whichever way the courts move."
]},
]
